  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data view (`kibana_sample_data_logs`)**: time field `timestamp`. The useful fields are `clientip` (IP), `bytes` (number), `response` and `response.keyword` (the status code, stored as a string), `url`, `request`, `extension`, `geo.dest`, `host`, `agent`, and `machine.os`.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-ebfad183 Web logs overview`** (id `01385a08-2eaa-467d-9947-563b2fcd4891`, last 7 days)\n- Four metric panels: Total requests, Unique client IPs, Average response size (bytes), and 5xx responses.\n- Requests over time (line chart).\n- Requests by response code, top 10 (bar chart).\n\nThe panels are ES|QL-backed. I read the dashboard back and confirmed all six panels saved.\n\n**Rule: `EFF-ebfad183 5xx spike`** (id `fbd8dcf0-01ec-4d27-9ee6-56b61eb75b67`)\n- Type: Elasticsearch query rule (`.es-query`), count aggregation, no grouping.\n- Query filter: `response.keyword` matches `5??`, which is any three-digit code starting with 5.\n- Condition: more than 10 hits over a 5-minute window. Runs every 1 minute.\n- Enabled, with no actions attached. The first run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- Consumer is `stackAlerts`.\n- The 5xx match uses a wildcard on `response.keyword` rather than an explicit list of codes.\n- No notification action, since none was requested.\n\n**Not verified:**\n- I could not run the ES|QL queries or count the matching documents directly. The console proxy, the ES|QL endpoint, and Elasticsearch on port 9200 were all unavailable, so I haven't checked the panel values or the rule's hit counts.\n- I didn't open the dashboard in the UI, so the charts are unchecked visually.",
